<!--
    RE-friction round 1, challenge 69a54bd70f5b9757a6a5f72f (PE x86-64
    PolyMLP.exe): `kuna decompile ... 0x1400024a0` by address renders the same
    allocator wrapper with two different arities thirty bytes apart,

        v13 = sub_140008160(0x28);          0x14000252c
        v13 = sub_140008160();              0x1400026b0
        v39 = (char *)sub_140008160(v33);   0x1400026cf

    destroying the buffer size dataflow at the site that lost it.

    Root cause (P4, trial finalization).  The odd site is MSVC's aligned
    operator new[], where the requested size is also the operand of an
    overflow check:

        lea  rcx, [rbx+0x27]
        cmp  rcx, rbx
        jbe  abort
        call sub_140008160

    Funcdata::onlyOpUse (funcdata_varnode.cc:1851) asks whether the Varnode
    reaching the CALL in rcx is used ONLY as this call's argument, and rejects
    on the CPUI_CBRANCH among its descendants; checkInputTrialUse then marks the
    trial inactive, which also sets CHECKED, so the argument is gone for good.
    That rule is upstream's and it is not wrong on its own terms: a register that
    is compared and branched on may be live caller state.  Relaxing it would
    fabricate an argument at every `test rcx,rcx; jz; call` in the corpus.  What
    settles the question is the OTHER call site, which passes rcx as an argument
    to the same callee.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "trial-finalization".  buildInputFromTrials
    reconciles a call's argument list with an already-final sibling call to the
    same entry address (kuna_calleearity.rs, always on).

    The fixture is the same construct on SysV x86-64 (rdi rather than rcx),
    assembled from:

        callee: mov %rdi,%rax ; add $1,%rax ; ret
        caller: push %rbx ; mov $0x28,%edi
                call callee            # site A: constant argument, no other use
                mov %rax,%rbx ; lea 0x27(%rbx),%rdi
                cmp %rbx,%rdi ; jbe bail
                call callee            # site B: the guard branches on %rdi
                pop %rbx ; ret
        bail:   xor %eax,%eax ; pop %rbx ; ret

    Site A has to pass a CONSTANT rather than a forwarded parameter: onlyOpUse
    walks a Varnode's whole descendant tree, so a register the caller also
    copies into the guarded expression is rejected at BOTH sites and there is no
    witness left to reconcile against.

    Site B renders `callee()` without the reconciliation; at the shipped
    default both sites pass an argument.  (The callee reads rdi on entry, so the
    callee-read rule keeps site B's argument too.)
-->
